(function(){'use strict';const{register,table,diagram}=window.AcademyContent;
register({id:67,centralQuestion:'Which decision should change, and is AI actually the least costly reliable way to change it?',objective:'Frame a business problem as a decision workflow; compare rules, analytics, process repair and AI using data readiness, actionability and error costs.',sections:[
{id:'decision',title:'Begin with an intervention, not a model',body:['A useful problem statement identifies who makes which decision, when it is made, what information exists then, and what outcome should improve. “Use AI for retention” is incomplete. “Prioritize 500 weekly outreach calls to customers likely to leave, where outreach can change their behavior” defines a constrained intervention. Prediction of churn alone does not establish who benefits from a call.','Separate prediction from action. A model can rank risk accurately while recommending an ineffective intervention. If high-risk customers have already decided to leave, targeting them may waste capacity; a lower-risk but persuadable group can generate more incremental value. Evaluation must connect model outputs to a feasible workflow and causal outcome measurement.','List alternatives before selecting AI: deterministic rules, better search, data cleanup, process redesign, human triage or no change. Rules often win when logic is stable and explicit; ML helps when patterns are complex and learnable; generative models help when flexible language work is valuable and errors can be controlled.'],table:table('Suitability decision',['Condition','Likely starting point','Reason'],[['Stable explicit policy','Rules/workflow','Deterministic and auditable'],['Repeated complex pattern with outcomes','Predictive ML baseline','Can learn from representative evidence'],['Document search need','Search/retrieval first','Generation may be unnecessary'],['No usable data or action owner','Data/process readiness work','Model cannot repair ownership'],['High-consequence unverifiable output','Constrained human workflow','Errors require control']]),check:{question:'Why is high churn prediction accuracy not proof of campaign value?',answer:'The intervention may not change those customers’ behavior; incremental retention and capacity matter.'}},
{id:'readiness',title:'Data availability is not data readiness',body:['Ask whether historical examples represent the future decision population, labels match the desired outcome, features exist at action time and rights permit the proposed use. Large datasets can still have proxy labels, selective observation or missing critical groups. A feasibility review should expose these constraints before a polished demo creates false confidence.','Define a baseline and minimum useful improvement. If a simple rule already captures most benefit at negligible cost, a model needs enough additional value to justify integration and operation. Include fallback and error consequences: a mistaken recommendation is different from an irreversible autonomous action.','Specify who owns the decision and can change the workflow. Without an accountable adopter, model output may become an unused dashboard. Feasibility includes staffing, systems integration, permissions and monitoring, not just whether training runs.'],failure:'Selecting an LLM because the request contains text can miss a simpler indexed search or form-validation solution with better reliability and lower cost.',check:{question:'What makes a labeled dataset unsuitable despite its size?',answer:'Unrepresentative sampling, wrong/proxy outcomes, unavailable-at-decision features, poor label quality or lack of permitted use.'}},
{id:'experiment',title:'Write a falsifiable value hypothesis',body:['State the proposed change, affected users, expected benefit, guardrails, time horizon and stop condition. For example, assisted drafting should reduce median handling time by 15% without lowering resolution quality or exposing private records. These are project assumptions, not certification thresholds.','Run a small, representative pilot with a control or credible comparison. Measure adoption and actual task completion, not only model scores. Separate learning goals from rollout commitments so a negative result can lead to stopping or choosing a simpler approach.','Communicate uncertainty plainly. AI suitability is a decision under evidence: sometimes the professional recommendation is to repair the process, gather data or avoid automation. That conclusion is productive when it prevents an expensive system with no actionable value.'],example:'A warehouse asks for vision AI to read damaged labels. An audit finds most failures come from poor printer maintenance. Repairing printers may remove the problem more cheaply; a residual difficult subset can then justify vision assistance.',check:{question:'What should a pilot be allowed to conclude?',answer:'That the intervention is not useful or ready, and that stopping or choosing a simpler alternative is appropriate.'}}
],glossary:[['Problem framing','Defining the decision, users, evidence, action and desired outcome.'],['AI suitability','Fit between a task’s needs and AI’s evidence, capability and controllability.'],['Actionability','Whether a prediction can lead to a feasible useful intervention.'],['Baseline','Current or simple alternative used for comparison.'],['Value hypothesis','Testable claim linking a proposed change to benefit and guardrails.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Decide whether to build churn AI',brief:'A company can call 500 customers weekly and has two years of churn labels, but no evidence that calls prevent churn.',parts:['Write the decision and action constraints.','Assess data/label timing and alternative rules.','Design a pilot for incremental retention.','State proceed/stop criteria and owners.'],solution:'Rank actionable outreach candidates rather than only high risk. Verify as-of features and label quality, compare a simple rule, and randomize eligible outreach where feasible to measure incremental retention net of call cost. Define capacity, complaints and privacy guardrails, an accountable campaign owner and a stop condition if calls do not change outcomes.'},sources:{core:['googleFraming','googlePeopleAI'],deep:['azureWellArchitectedAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Actionability',prompt:'A churn model finds customers who cannot be retained by any available offer. Why might value remain low?',answer:0,options:[['Prediction is not the same as effective intervention.','The action cannot alter the outcome.'],['AUC always equals profit.','It does not.'],['More features guarantee retention.','The intervention remains ineffective.'],['Calls have no cost.','Capacity and costs matter.']]},
{concept:'AI suitability',prompt:'A stable eligibility policy is fully specified in ten rules. What should be compared first?',answer:1,options:[['The largest LLM only.','Complexity may be unnecessary.'],['A deterministic rules baseline.','It may be reliable and auditable.'],['No implementation at all by default.','The task may be valuable.'],['Fine-tuning before requirements.','That reverses the decision process.']]},
{concept:'Data readiness',prompt:'Millions of labels record whether agents reviewed a case, not whether fraud occurred. What is the issue?',answer:2,options:[['Too few rows by definition.','Volume is large.'],['The labels are guaranteed correct targets.','They describe process selection.'],['Proxy labels may learn reviewer behavior rather than fraud.','Target semantics matter.'],['All proxy labels are forbidden.','They can be useful with validated limitations.']]},
{concept:'Alternatives',prompt:'Most OCR failures are caused by a broken printer. What should the team evaluate?',answer:3,options:[['Only a larger model.','The upstream cause may be cheaper to repair.'],['Ignore hardware maintenance.','It causes the failures.'],['Train on test examples until perfect.','That contaminates evidence.'],['Process/equipment repair before or alongside AI.','Addressing the cause may remove demand.']]},
{concept:'Pilot value',prompt:'Drafting assistance reduces generation time but agents spend longer correcting it. Which metric matters?',answer:0,options:[['Total handling time and resolution quality.','The whole workflow determines value.'],['Only token speed.','It omits correction.'],['Only model size.','Size is not benefit.'],['Only number of drafts.','Volume may be waste.']]},
{concept:'Ownership',prompt:'A model is accurate but nobody can change the operational workflow. What feasibility gap remains?',answer:1,options:[['Only learning rate.','The model already works.'],['An accountable action/adoption owner.','Predictions need a workflow decision.'],['Only GPU memory.','Unrelated.'],['No gap if a dashboard exists.','A dashboard may be unused.']]},
{concept:'Stop criteria',prompt:'A controlled pilot shows no incremental benefit after costs. What is a valid outcome?',answer:2,options:[['Roll out to justify sunk cost.','Past spending does not create future value.'],['Hide the result.','That prevents informed decisions.'],['Stop, revise the intervention or choose a simpler option.','Negative evidence should change the decision.'],['Claim an official certification failure.','Project outcomes are unrelated to official exam results.']]}
]});
register({id:68,centralQuestion:'Whose metric decides success when model quality, reviewer workload and customer outcomes disagree?',objective:'Translate stakeholder requirements into technical metrics, business KPIs, guardrails and acceptance criteria with owners and measurement definitions.',sections:[
{id:'stakeholders',title:'Requirements include people affected by the decision',body:['Stakeholders include users, affected customers, operations, data owners, security, risk, finance, platform and support. Their goals may conflict: higher recall increases review load, lower latency may increase cost, and aggressive automation may reduce recourse. Gather the decision context and constraints before negotiating a single success number.','A requirement should state behavior and evidence: who can access which data, what latency percentile applies, how errors are handled, which tasks require approval, and what happens when evidence is missing. “Accurate and secure” cannot be tested without definitions.','Separate must-pass constraints from optimization goals. A privacy boundary or critical safety floor should not be averaged away by a gain in clicks. Owners must agree on how conflicting goals are resolved and who can accept residual risk.'],check:{question:'Why distinguish guardrails from optimization metrics?',answer:'Some constraints must hold even when the primary KPI improves; averaging them together can hide unacceptable harm.'}},
{id:'metric-chain',title:'Connect technical behavior to operational and business outcomes',body:['Technical metrics describe model or system behavior: precision, recall, calibration, latency and availability. Operational metrics describe workload: alerts per day, review time, overrides and completion. Business KPIs describe value and harm: loss avoided, retained revenue, downtime, customer resolution or complaint rate. The chain must be explicit.','For fraud, lowering a threshold can increase recall and alerts. If review capacity is fixed, queues grow and delayed investigations may reduce prevented loss. Precision helps estimate false-positive burden, but value also depends on fraud amount, investigation cost and action timing.','Define numerator, denominator, unit, population, window, source and owner for every KPI. Distinguish eligible users from all visitors and mature outcomes from incomplete labels. Without shared definitions, two dashboards can disagree while both calculations are internally correct.'],table:table('Fraud metric chain',['Level','Metric','Decision use'],[['Model','Recall/precision/calibration','Which cases are detected and how reliable scores are'],['Operations','Alerts, review minutes, queue age','Can staff act in time?'],['Business','Net prevented loss','Does action exceed total cost?'],['Guardrail','Wrongful blocks/critical slice harm','Is the intervention acceptable?']]),failure:'A project can hit an AUC target and fail because no threshold satisfies both review capacity and required recall. Acceptance must include the operating point.',check:{question:'What extra evidence is needed beyond AUC for launch?',answer:'A feasible threshold with costs, capacity, calibration, slice behavior and operational outcomes.'}},
{id:'acceptance',title:'Turn requirements into observable acceptance decisions',body:['Use concrete scenarios: invalid requests are rejected before inference; revoked users cannot retrieve cached private answers; a failed replica does not break p99; unsupported questions abstain. Pair each with a measurement and an owner.','Measurement plans should specify baseline, rollout comparison, uncertainty and time horizon. Avoid changing the success criterion after seeing results merely to declare victory. If new evidence changes priorities, record the decision and its consequences.','Communicate at the right level. Engineers need contracts, thresholds and failure paths; business owners need outcome, cost, uncertainty and choices. Use the same evidence with different detail rather than presenting incompatible stories.'],example:'“Reduce review cost by 20%” is incomplete. Define cost per resolved case, include model and correction costs, preserve a quality floor, choose a representative period and assign an operations owner to confirm actual staffing effects.',check:{question:'Why can automation time savings fail to become cash savings?',answer:'Saved minutes may not reduce staffing or enable valuable additional work; realization depends on workflow and capacity decisions.'}}
],glossary:[['Stakeholder analysis','Identifying goals, influence, obligations and affected groups.'],['Business KPI','Measure of organizational value or harm.'],['Guardrail metric','Constraint that must remain acceptable during optimization.'],['Acceptance criterion','Observable condition required for approval.'],['Metric definition','Numerator, denominator, population, window, source and unit.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Negotiate fraud launch criteria',brief:'Data science wants recall, finance wants net loss reduction, operations can review 1,000 alerts daily and support fears wrongful blocks.',parts:['Map stakeholders and decisions.','Write a technical-to-business metric chain.','Define capacity and harm guardrails.','Create five measurable acceptance scenarios.'],solution:'Evaluate thresholds on representative mature data, estimate alert volume/review minutes and net prevented loss after false-positive and service costs. Keep wrongful-block and critical-slice floors as gates. Assign data science, operations, finance and risk owners; test overload, abstention, authorization and rollback in addition to predictive quality.'},sources:{core:['googleFraming','googlePeopleAI'],deep:['sklearnMetrics']},quiz:[
{concept:'Metric chain',prompt:'Recall rises but review queues delay action until fraud losses occur. What was missing?',answer:0,options:[['Operational capacity and action timing in the success model.','Detection alone does not prevent loss.'],['Only more epochs.','The workflow is limiting.'],['Only accuracy.','It would not measure queue delay.'],['No human involvement ever.','The workflow can be redesigned.']]},
{concept:'Guardrail',prompt:'Conversion improves while a privacy boundary fails. Should the metrics be averaged into a pass?',answer:1,options:[['Yes, gains cancel disclosure.','A critical constraint cannot be traded silently.'],['No; the privacy gate must hold independently.','Guardrails constrain optimization.'],['Yes if conversion is larger.','Units and consequences differ.'],['Only hide the privacy metric.','That removes accountability.']]},
{concept:'Metric definition',prompt:'Two teams report different completion rates using different eligible populations. What should be aligned?',answer:2,options:[['Only chart colors.','The denominator differs.'],['Only model name.','It does not define eligibility.'],['Population, numerator, denominator, window and source.','Shared semantics enable comparison.'],['Only the number of dashboards.','Count is irrelevant.']]},
{concept:'Acceptance',prompt:'AUC passes but no threshold meets recall and review-capacity limits. Is deployment evidence sufficient?',answer:3,options:[['Yes, AUC guarantees a feasible point.','It does not.'],['Yes, capacity can be ignored.','Operations cannot act on excess alerts.'],['Yes, use threshold 0.5 automatically.','That may violate both constraints.'],['No; operating-point feasibility is part of acceptance.','Ranking alone is insufficient.']]},
{concept:'Time savings',prompt:'An assistant saves minutes but staffing and output remain unchanged. What should finance avoid claiming?',answer:0,options:[['Realized cash savings without a mechanism.','Time savings need conversion into value.'],['Any productivity observation.','Time can still be measured.'],['Any operational metric.','Those remain useful.'],['Any pilot at all.','Pilots can test realization.']]},
{concept:'Requirements',prompt:'“Secure and fast” appears as the only acceptance requirement. What is needed?',answer:1,options:[['A larger font.','It remains untestable.'],['Concrete access, latency percentile, failure and test conditions.','Requirements need observable evidence.'],['A claim of official approval.','Not justified.'],['Only more features.','That does not define success.']]},
{concept:'Communication',prompt:'Executives need a rollout decision while engineers need failure details. What is the best approach?',answer:2,options:[['Give contradictory conclusions.','Evidence should be consistent.'],['Hide uncertainty from executives.','It affects decisions.'],['Use the same evidence with audience-appropriate detail and explicit choices.','This preserves accuracy and usefulness.'],['Send only raw logs to everyone.','That obscures the decision.']]}
]});
register({id:69,centralQuestion:'Where does the total cost hide when a vendor demo makes the model look inexpensive?',objective:'Compare build, buy and hybrid options using feasibility, data readiness, TCO, ROI, risk, portability and vendor evidence.',sections:[
{id:'cost',title:'Count the lifecycle, including failed and idle work',body:['Total cost of ownership includes discovery, data preparation, integration, training/tuning, evaluation, inference, storage, network, observability, security, on-call, support, change and retirement. Vendor tokens or GPU hours are only part. Include human review and correction, idle reserve and incident remediation.','ROI commonly compares net benefit with investment, but define the horizon and included costs. A simple project estimate might be (benefit−cost)/cost. Cash flows over multiple years may require discounted analysis and finance review. Avoid false precision: adoption, task success, demand and error cost often dominate uncertainty.','Benefits must be incremental against a credible baseline. Gross revenue handled by a system is not revenue caused by it. Time saved is not automatically cash saved. Use sensitivity ranges and break-even analysis to identify which assumptions can reverse the decision.'],table:table('Option comparison',['Option','Strength','Hidden obligation'],[['Build','Control and customization','Staffing, operations and maintenance'],['Buy','Faster capability access','Usage cost, limits, vendor dependency'],['Hybrid','Specialist service plus owned workflow','Integration and split responsibility'],['Abstain/defer','Avoid unjustified cost/risk','Opportunity cost and readiness work']]),check:{question:'Why is vendor usage price not TCO?',answer:'Integration, evaluation, review, operations, data, reliability and exit costs remain.'}},
{id:'vendor',title:'Evaluate the service promise and the exit path',body:['Test vendors on representative protected data or approved synthetic cases, quality slices, latency tails, rate limits, reliability, security controls, data handling, version changes and support. A polished demo may use curated examples and warm caches. Ask what evidence is reproducible under your workload.','Contract and technical review should clarify data retention/use, access, regions, incident response, model-change policy, quotas, portability and export. These are due-diligence questions, not claims about a particular provider or legal requirements. Involve qualified procurement/security/legal owners where appropriate.','Avoid lock-in surprises by separating business logic from provider adapters and preserving evaluation sets, prompts, schemas and data in usable formats. Portability is not free: different models have different tokenizers, outputs and safety behavior. Test the fallback provider rather than assuming an API-compatible route is behaviorally equivalent.'],failure:'Choosing the cheapest advertised token rate can be more expensive if the model needs longer prompts, more retries or human correction to complete the same task.',check:{question:'Why does API compatibility not guarantee safe vendor failover?',answer:'Quality, tool behavior, tokenization, limits and safety characteristics can differ despite similar request schemas.'}},
{id:'decision',title:'Make uncertainty and ownership explicit',body:['Assess technical feasibility, data readiness, production readiness and organizational capability separately. A team may prove the model works yet lack on-call capacity or permission to use the required data. Buying a service does not transfer responsibility for the application’s decisions.','Use a weighted comparison only after must-pass constraints are checked. A vendor that fails a critical access or reliability requirement should not win by scoring highly on cosmetic features. Record assumptions, evidence, decision owners and revisit triggers.','A pilot should test the biggest uncertain value/risk assumptions before a long commitment. If adoption is the largest uncertainty, another model benchmark may add little decision value. If data rights or security are unresolved, resolve those gates before expanding exposure.'],example:'Option A costs less per token but completes 60% of tasks; B costs twice as much per call but completes 95% with little correction. Compare total cost per successful task, including retries and reviewer minutes, before choosing.',check:{question:'Which uncertainty should a pilot target?',answer:'The assumption most likely to change the decision, whether quality, adoption, cost, data readiness or risk.'}}
],glossary:[['TCO','Lifecycle cost of owning and operating the solution.'],['ROI','Return relative to investment under a stated formula and horizon.'],['Break-even','Point where incremental benefits equal relevant costs.'],['Vendor lock-in','Cost or difficulty of changing suppliers.'],['Feasibility','Ability to deliver within technical, data, operational and organizational constraints.']],exercise:{duration:40,title:'Build a buy-versus-build decision sheet',brief:'A vendor costs 0.04 per request; internal serving estimates 0.015, but needs two engineers and on-call. Use clearly labeled hypothetical currency units.',parts:['List full lifecycle costs and success-adjusted usage.','Define must-pass vendor gates.','Vary volume, completion and review time.','Write a decision and exit/revisit plan.'],solution:'Add staffing, review, evaluation, integration, storage, observability and idle/recovery capacity to both options. Compare cost per successful task over the same horizon and SLO. Gate security/data handling/quality before weighted preferences. Pilot the uncertain completion/adoption assumptions, retain portable contracts/evaluation and test an exit path.'},sources:{core:['googleFraming','azureWellArchitectedAI'],deep:['googlePeopleAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'TCO',prompt:'Internal inference is cheaper per call but needs two engineers. Which comparison is appropriate?',answer:0,options:[['Full lifecycle cost at the same success and service promise.','Staffing can dominate unit compute savings.'],['Compute price only.','It omits ownership.'],['Vendor logo recognition.','Not a cost model.'],['Number of model parameters only.','It is not total cost.']]},
{concept:'Incremental benefit',prompt:'A platform handles 10 million in revenue. Can all of it be claimed as AI-created benefit?',answer:1,options:[['Yes, handled means caused.','That confuses throughput and incrementality.'],['No; compare outcomes against a credible baseline.','Only additional value is attributable.'],['Yes if the model is accurate.','Accuracy does not establish causation.'],['No business benefit can be measured.','Controlled comparisons can estimate it.']]},
{concept:'Vendor evaluation',prompt:'A demo is fast on five curated examples. What is needed before selection?',answer:2,options:[['Only the demo video.','It may not represent your workload.'],['Only public popularity.','That does not establish fit.'],['Representative quality, tail latency, limits, data and operational tests.','Selection needs reproducible evidence.'],['No pilot.','A pilot can reduce uncertainty.']]},
{concept:'Hard gates',prompt:'A vendor fails required tenant isolation but scores highly on features. What should happen?',answer:3,options:[['Average the failure away.','A must-pass boundary remains failed.'],['Choose it for features.','That ignores a critical constraint.'],['Hide the requirement.','That evades accountability.'],['Reject or remediate the failed gate before comparison.','Constraints precede preference scoring.']]},
{concept:'Portability',prompt:'A fallback provider accepts the same API schema. Is behavior equivalence established?',answer:0,options:[['No; evaluate quality, safety, limits and tools.','Schema compatibility is only one layer.'],['Yes, every model is identical.','They differ.'],['Yes if endpoint names match.','Names do not prove behavior.'],['No adapters can be built.','Adapters are useful but need tests.']]},
{concept:'ROI assumptions',prompt:'ROI is positive only at 90% adoption; pilot adoption is 20%. What should change?',answer:1,options:[['Keep the original claim unchanged.','Evidence invalidates a key assumption.'],['Update sensitivity and adoption/workflow assumptions before rollout.','The value case may reverse.'],['Delete pilot data.','That removes evidence.'],['Increase context length automatically.','It may not address adoption.']]},
{concept:'Responsibility',prompt:'A vendor supplies the model. Who remains responsible for application workflow and decisions?',answer:2,options:[['Nobody.','Ownership cannot disappear.'],['Only the model API.','It does not own the full workflow.'],['The deploying organization’s accountable product, technical and risk owners.','Buying capability does not transfer all responsibility.'],['Only end users without support.','The system owner still has obligations.']]}
]});
register({id:70,centralQuestion:'What evidence turns an impressive proof of concept into a service people can depend on?',objective:'Distinguish PoC, pilot, MVP and production rollout; define stage-specific learning, gates, scope and operational readiness.',sections:[
{id:'stages',title:'Stages answer different questions',body:['A proof of concept tests whether a technical approach can work under limited conditions. It may use curated data and manual steps. A pilot tests usefulness and feasibility with representative users and controlled exposure. An MVP is the smallest usable product that delivers value and tests assumptions; “minimum” does not excuse missing essential security or reliability.','Production rollout exposes an owned, supported service at a defined scale. It requires contracts, monitoring, release controls, incident response, documentation, capacity and data governance. A notebook that predicts accurately is evidence for one stage, not the whole lifecycle.','Define the learning goal and exit criterion for each stage before starting. Otherwise a PoC can drift into unowned production when users begin depending on it. Track who uses it and what consequences it causes even during limited trials.'],table:table('Stage evidence',['Stage','Question','Exit evidence'],[['PoC','Can the mechanism work?','Technical feasibility on stated data'],['Pilot','Does it help representative users safely?','Workflow value, risks and adoption'],['MVP','Can a minimal product deliver useful service?','Usable scope with essential controls'],['Production','Can it operate at promised scale?','Quality, security, SLO, ownership and recovery']]),check:{question:'Why is an MVP not permission to omit authorization?',answer:'Minimum scope can exclude features, but essential data/action boundaries are required for safe use.'}},
{id:'readiness',title:'Production readiness is a dependency graph',body:['Check artifact reproducibility, data contracts, model evaluation, API compatibility, load behavior, privacy/security, accessibility, support and ownership. Each criterion needs evidence and an accountable owner. Missing one can defeat otherwise strong work: a correct model without a stable feature feed is not deployable.','Pilot users should represent the eventual population and workload. Internal enthusiasts may tolerate errors and know workarounds that ordinary users do not. Include difficult slices, realistic task lengths and failure cases. Measure correction and review effort.','Design rollback and stop criteria before expansion. A canary limits exposure but does not replace evaluation. Record release identities and preserve the prior compatible stack so a failed rollout can be reversed.'],failure:'A prototype quietly used by customers for months has production consequences even if its repository still says “demo.” Ownership follows actual reliance.',check:{question:'What makes a pilot representative?',answer:'Users, tasks, data, workload and failure conditions resemble the intended deployment population, including difficult slices.'}},
{id:'rollout',title:'Increase exposure only when the next uncertainty is bounded',body:['Start with a controlled cohort and observe quality, adoption, operations and harm. Expand by predeclared gates and adequate time windows. A spike in usage can mean interest, confusion or repeated failed attempts; pair activity with task completion and user outcomes.','Plan training, support channels, feedback triage and handover before launch. Users need to understand automation limits and recovery paths. Support teams need version-aware runbooks and escalation routes.','A production-readiness review should end with a concrete decision: approve a bounded scope, require specific remediation, or stop. Avoid a vague “mostly ready” label that leaves critical gaps unowned.'],example:'A document assistant passes technical evaluation but users cannot tell which policy version it cites. A pilot reveals this trust problem; source/version display and escalation may be required before broad rollout.',check:{question:'What should a readiness review produce?',answer:'An explicit approved scope or blocked items with owners and evidence needed, plus stop and rollback conditions.'}}
],glossary:[['PoC','Limited test of technical feasibility.'],['Pilot','Controlled real-user test of value and readiness.'],['MVP','Smallest usable product delivering value with essential controls.'],['Production readiness','Evidence that a service can be safely owned and operated at scope.'],['Progressive rollout','Expansion of exposure through measured gates.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Promote a notebook assistant responsibly',brief:'A notebook demo is now used by 30 employees for policy decisions; there is no owner or rollback.',parts:['Identify its actual operational status.','Define PoC/pilot/production evidence gaps.','Write bounded pilot gates and representative cohorts.','Create ownership, support and rollback requirements.'],solution:'Treat current reliance as a production risk even if labeled demo. Assign an owner, restrict scope and communicate limits. Validate sources, access, quality, versions and representative tasks; add telemetry, support, rollback and stop triggers. Approve expansion only after explicit gaps are closed with evidence.'},sources:{core:['googlePeopleAI','azureWellArchitectedAI'],deep:['googleFraming']},quiz:[
{concept:'PoC scope',prompt:'A PoC works on 20 curated examples. What has it established?',answer:0,options:[['Limited technical feasibility under stated conditions.','It does not establish production readiness.'],['All-domain production safety.','Evidence is too narrow.'],['Guaranteed adoption.','Users were not evaluated.'],['An official exam result.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'MVP',prompt:'A team omits authorization because the product is an MVP. Is this justified?',answer:1,options:[['Yes, minimum means no controls.','Essential boundaries still apply.'],['No; reduce scope while retaining essential protection.','Minimal features do not excuse unsafe access.'],['Yes if there are few users.','Private data remains private.'],['Yes if the model is accurate.','Accuracy is not authorization.']]},
{concept:'Pilot population',prompt:'Only expert internal enthusiasts test a public assistant. What risk remains?',answer:2,options:[['No risk because experts approved.','Their behavior may differ.'],['Only model-file size.','Not the representativeness issue.'],['Ordinary users and difficult tasks may expose adoption/failure gaps.','Pilot coverage should match deployment.'],['No feedback is useful.','Feedback is useful when interpreted correctly.']]},
{concept:'Shadow production',prompt:'Customers rely daily on an app labeled demo with no on-call owner. What matters?',answer:3,options:[['The label removes consequences.','Reliance creates real risk.'],['Only the repository name.','Names do not determine use.'],['No monitoring is needed.','Failures affect users.'],['Actual use requires ownership and operational controls.','Responsibility follows consequence.']]},
{concept:'Rollout gate',prompt:'Usage doubles but completion falls and support tickets rise. Should expansion be automatic?',answer:0,options:[['No; inspect value and friction before expanding.','Activity alone is ambiguous.'],['Yes, usage always equals success.','Retries/confusion can drive usage.'],['Yes, ignore tickets.','They signal workflow problems.'],['Delete the completion metric.','That hides evidence.']]},
{concept:'Readiness decision',prompt:'A review ends “mostly ready” with no gap owners. What is missing?',answer:1,options:[['Only a celebratory message.','The decision remains vague.'],['Explicit approved scope, blockers, owners and required evidence.','This makes readiness actionable.'],['A larger model by default.','Gaps may be operational.'],['No review at all.','Review is useful when concrete.']]},
{concept:'Rollback',prompt:'A pilot expands without preserving the prior compatible release. What risk increases?',answer:2,options:[['Only font inconsistency.','The issue is recovery.'],['Automatic better accuracy.','No causal basis.'],['Time and uncertainty to restore safe behavior after failure.','Rollback must exist before exposure.'],['No risk if deployment succeeds.','Failures can appear later.']]}
]});
register({id:71,centralQuestion:'Why do people avoid a technically successful model, and what would make its use worth their effort?',objective:'Design adoption, change management, human-in-the-loop workflows, feedback triage and cross-functional communication around real user work.',sections:[
{id:'workflow',title:'Adoption depends on the whole task',body:['Users compare the new workflow with their current way of getting work done. A model may save drafting time but add verification, copying, context entry or correction. Observe the full task, including edge cases and handoffs, rather than optimizing one inference step.','Trust should be calibrated, not maximized blindly. Users need clear capabilities, uncertainty, sources, failure signals and recourse. Overtrust can cause automation bias; undertrust can make a useful tool irrelevant. Show evidence where it changes decisions and avoid confident presentation unsupported by quality.','Human-in-the-loop design specifies who reviews what, at what time, with which authority and capacity. Routing every output to review can erase efficiency and overwhelm staff. Use risk-based review and safe abstention while ensuring high-consequence cases receive qualified attention.'],check:{question:'Why can faster generation reduce productivity?',answer:'Verification, correction and handoff costs can exceed the time saved in generation.'}},
{id:'feedback',title:'Feedback is evidence, not automatic truth',body:['Provide simple ways to flag errors, correct outputs and explain impact. Capture task, release, source evidence and outcome under privacy controls. A thumbs-down alone does not distinguish factual error, bad retrieval, style preference or workflow failure.','Triage feedback with model, product, data, platform and risk owners. Deduplicate incidents, prioritize by severity and affected volume, and close the loop with users. Do not feed every correction directly into training: malicious or mistaken feedback can poison the target.','Measure adoption by eligible users and successful tasks, not logins alone. Track abandonment, override, correction time, escalation and subgroup experience. A release may improve model scores while worsening usability for a particular language or accessibility need.'],table:table('Feedback to action',['Signal','Possible issue','Owner/evidence'],[['Wrong citation','Retrieval/context lineage','Model/data team with source IDs'],['Long correction time','Output/task mismatch','Product + domain users'],['Access complaint','Permission/workflow issue','Security + product'],['Slow response','Serving/dependency tail','Platform traces'],['Unsafe action','Policy/tool boundary','Incident and risk owners']]),failure:'Treating every negative rating as a correct training label can amplify frustration, ambiguity or malicious input instead of improving the system.',check:{question:'What context makes user feedback actionable?',answer:'Task, version, evidence, expected behavior, actual impact and privacy-safe reproduction details.'}},
{id:'change',title:'Communication and handover are part of implementation',body:['Explain the change in terms of the user’s task: what improves, what still requires judgment and how to recover. Provide training with realistic failures, not only perfect demonstrations. Establish support and escalation before broad adoption.','Technical stakeholders need contracts, versions, metrics and runbooks. Business stakeholders need value, cost, risk and choices. Cross-functional decisions should record disagreements and accepted trade-offs so future teams understand why the workflow looks as it does.','Change management continues after launch. Compare adoption cohorts, listen to affected users and revise the workflow when evidence warrants it. A model replacement may require retraining staff or changing escalation policy even when the API remains identical.'],example:'Nurses ignore a triage score because it appears after they already decide and offers no explanation. Moving it earlier with evidence and a clear override path may improve usefulness more than another point of AUC.',check:{question:'Why can unchanged API shape still require user change management?',answer:'Behavior, uncertainty, explanations or escalation needs can change when the model/prompt changes.'}}
],glossary:[['Adoption','Sustained useful use by the intended population.'],['Automation bias','Overreliance on automated suggestions despite contrary evidence.'],['Human-in-the-loop','Workflow with defined human review/intervention points.'],['Feedback triage','Classification and prioritization of user evidence for action.'],['Change management','Preparing and supporting people through a workflow transition.']],exercise:{duration:35,title:'Repair a low-adoption assistant',brief:'An accurate assistant is used by 15% of eligible staff; users report copying overhead and distrust of citations.',parts:['Map the end-to-end task and friction.','Define calibrated-trust UI evidence and review roles.','Design feedback categories and owners.','Choose adoption and successful-task metrics for a pilot.'],solution:'Observe full workflows, integrate output where work happens and make source/version evidence inspectable. Define when users verify or escalate and fit review to capacity. Capture task/version/error category, triage to product/data/model/platform owners and validate corrections before training. Measure eligible-user adoption, completion, correction time and quality rather than logins.'},sources:{core:['googlePeopleAI','googleFraming'],deep:['microsoftRAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Workflow productivity',prompt:'Drafting is 30 seconds faster but correction takes two extra minutes. What should be optimized?',answer:0,options:[['End-to-end successful task time and quality.','The net workflow is slower.'],['Generation speed alone.','It hides correction cost.'],['Only token count.','It is not task value.'],['Only number of logins.','Use is not completion.']]},
{concept:'Calibrated trust',prompt:'A system hides uncertainty to encourage adoption. What risk increases?',answer:1,options:[['Guaranteed appropriate reliance.','Users cannot judge limitations.'],['Automation bias and overtrust.','Confidence presentation can exceed evidence.'],['Only storage cost.','The primary issue is reliance.'],['No risk if UI is clean.','Appearance does not ensure truth.']]},
{concept:'Feedback quality',prompt:'Every thumbs-down is used as a negative training label. What is wrong?',answer:2,options:[['Ratings are always perfect labels.','They can reflect many causes.'],['No feedback should be collected.','Feedback is useful when interpreted.'],['Ambiguous or malicious feedback can corrupt the target.','Triage and validation are needed.'],['Only GPU count matters.','Unrelated.']]},
{concept:'Review capacity',prompt:'All 20,000 daily outputs require one minute of review, but staff have 500 minutes. What must change?',answer:3,options:[['Assume reviewers work faster without evidence.','Capacity mismatch remains.'],['Hide the backlog.','That does not solve it.'],['Call every click meaningful review.','Oversight quality collapses.'],['Scope automation/review by risk and provision feasible capacity.','The workflow must be operable.']]},
{concept:'Adoption metric',prompt:'Logins increase but tasks are abandoned. What does this indicate?',answer:0,options:[['Activity does not establish useful adoption.','Measure completion and friction.'],['Guaranteed business value.','Abandonment contradicts that.'],['Perfect model accuracy.','No quality evidence is given.'],['No need for user research.','Research can explain the gap.']]},
{concept:'Timing',prompt:'A triage recommendation arrives after staff make the decision. Why is its value limited?',answer:1,options:[['The model must be too small.','Timing is the issue.'],['It misses the decision point in the workflow.','Useful evidence must arrive when actionable.'],['Explanations never matter.','They may still help.'],['All triage AI is unsuitable.','The integration can be redesigned.']]},
{concept:'Cross-functional work',prompt:'Users report wrong citations and slow responses. Who should receive the evidence?',answer:2,options:[['Only finance with no technical context.','Different causes need different owners.'],['Nobody until retraining.','Diagnosis should precede repair.'],['Data/model and platform owners with task/version/source/trace context.','This separates retrieval and serving causes.'],['Only a generic rating counter.','That loses actionable detail.']]}
]});
register({id:72,centralQuestion:'When an AI incident crosses model, platform and business boundaries, who owns the next decision?',objective:'Establish operational ownership, handover, incident/ethical escalation, decision records and trade-off communication; complete a full 140-question mock under timed conditions.',sections:[
{id:'ownership',title:'Ownership follows decisions, not repository boundaries',body:['A production handover includes intended use, contracts, release identities, dashboards, SLOs, known limits, rollback, dependencies, credentials process and escalation. Uploading weights is not a handover. The receiving team must demonstrate it can operate and recover the service.','Assign decision authority explicitly: incident command coordinates; platform responders restore service; model/data owners diagnose behavior; business/risk owners decide consequential workflow changes and residual risk. One person may hold several roles in a small team, but the responsibilities still exist.','Separate incident urgency from organizational blame. During containment, the goal is safe restoration and evidence preservation. A blameless review later examines system conditions, missed controls and corrective actions with owners and verification.'],table:table('Operational responsibility',['Decision','Accountable role/evidence'],[['Contain harmful action','Incident commander + risk owner'],['Route/rollback release','Authorized platform/release owner'],['Assess prediction failure','Model/data owner with lineage'],['Remediate affected users','Business/support/risk owners'],['Accept residual risk','Designated accountable authority'],['Verify corrective action','Named owner with test evidence']]),check:{question:'What proves a handover worked?',answer:'The receiving team can locate evidence, operate the service, respond to an incident and execute recovery using the supplied runbooks.'}},
{id:'communication',title:'Communicate state, impact and the next decision',body:['An incident update should state observed impact, affected scope, current containment, uncertainty, owner and next update or decision. Avoid claiming root cause before evidence supports it. Distinguish service restoration from remediation of past decisions.','Technical communication includes release IDs, traces, hypotheses and tests. Business communication includes customer/workflow impact, cost, alternatives and residual risk. Both should reflect the same facts. Ethical escalation may require pausing expansion even when availability remains green.','Decision records preserve context, options, evidence, selected trade-off and revisit triggers. Record why a simpler fallback or a slower but safer workflow was chosen. Without this, future maintainers may remove an important control as apparent inefficiency.'],failure:'“The model team owns everything AI” leaves infrastructure, user remediation and risk acceptance unclear. Assign authority to concrete decisions rather than a technology label.',check:{question:'Why distinguish restoration and remediation?',answer:'Routing back can stop new harm, but past decisions, sent messages or affected users may still require corrective action.'}},
{id:'mock-protocol',title:'Use the full simulation to test pacing and decision discipline',body:['This session’s main practical work is a 140-question, 210-minute Certification-style Practice simulation. It is custom practice, not official SDAIA content. Use Full Mock A, start the timer, answer without revealing feedback, flag uncertainty and submit only when ready or when time expires. The bank-based forms may revisit lesson concepts and questions, so familiarity must be considered when interpreting scores.','The average allowance is 90 seconds per question. Use a first pass to answer well-understood scenarios and flag expensive uncertainty; return to flagged and unanswered items with the remaining time. Read the requested decision, constraints and failure evidence before selecting a familiar technique.','Reserve the final 30 minutes of the 240-minute session for initial analysis: record timing, confidence and dominant error mechanisms. Detailed repair continues in subsequent integrated sessions. A mock percentage is a study signal, never an invented official passing score or a guarantee of certification.'],check:{question:'What should be recorded besides the mock score?',answer:'Timing, flagged uncertainty, unanswered items, domain/subtopic errors and whether mistakes were conceptual, numerical or constraint-reading failures.'}}
],glossary:[['Handover','Transfer of operational capability and responsibility with evidence.'],['Incident commander','Role coordinating response and decisions during an incident.'],['Residual risk','Risk remaining after controls and mitigations.'],['Decision record','Documented options, evidence, choice, consequences and revisit triggers.'],['Remediation','Correction of effects remaining after containment/restoration.']],exercise:{duration:210,title:'Full Mock A — 140 questions in 210 minutes',brief:'Use the Full Mock A route. Total session is 240 minutes: 210 minutes simulation and 30 minutes first-pass analysis; consult this short chapter before starting.',parts:['Prepare an uninterrupted environment and begin the full timer.','Use answer navigation and flags without revealing answers.','Submit and inspect domain/subtopic breakdown.','Spend the remaining 30 minutes recording top error mechanisms and next repair actions.'],solution:'There is no fixed answer sequence in this chapter: the submitted mock provides an explanation for every option. Add wrong and flagged items to the mistake/review system. Separate knowledge gaps from arithmetic, overlooked constraints and premature technique selection. Prioritize recurring mechanisms across domains and retain the attempt record for comparison with Full Mock B.'},sources:{core:['googlePeopleAI','nistAIRMF'],deep:['azureWellArchitectedAI']},quiz:[
{concept:'Handover',prompt:'A team receives weights but no runbook, owner or rollback. What is missing?',answer:0,options:[['Operational capability and responsibility transfer.','Files alone are not a handover.'],['Only more parameters.','The gap is ownership.'],['Only a model logo.','Irrelevant.'],['Nothing if accuracy is high.','Operation can still fail.']]},
{concept:'Incident command',prompt:'Three teams make conflicting rollback changes during one incident. Which role should coordinate?',answer:1,options:[['No role; more changes always help.','They can obscure cause and worsen state.'],['A designated incident commander with clear decision authority.','Coordination limits conflicting actions.'],['Only the model output.','It is not accountable authority.'],['Only procurement.','Not necessarily the response owner.']]},
{concept:'Communication',prompt:'Root cause is uncertain but impact is known. What should an update say?',answer:2,options:[['Invent a confident root cause.','That misleads decisions.'],['Stay silent until complete certainty.','Stakeholders need impact and containment.'],['State impact, containment, uncertainty, owner and next decision/update.','It supports action without false certainty.'],['Publish raw private logs.','That can create disclosure.']]},
{concept:'Residual risk',prompt:'A workaround restores service but leaves a known subgroup quality gap. Who should accept it?',answer:3,options:[['Nobody; ignore it.','Risk remains.'],['Any anonymous developer.','Authority should be designated.'],['The dashboard automatically.','Metrics do not accept responsibility.'],['The accountable risk/business authority with evidence and scope.','Acceptance must be explicit.']]},
{concept:'Decision records',prompt:'A future engineer removes a slow approval step without knowing why it exists. What documentation helps prevent this?',answer:0,options:[['A record of the risk, alternatives and reason for the control.','It preserves the decision context.'],['Only a function name.','It may not explain consequence.'],['Only model accuracy.','The control may protect actions.'],['No documentation to reduce clutter.','That loses institutional reasoning.']]},
{concept:'Ethical escalation',prompt:'Availability is green but a group is systematically denied service. Is escalation justified?',answer:1,options:[['No, only outages matter.','Harm can occur in a healthy service.'],['Yes; ethical/quality risk has its own accountable path.','System uptime is insufficient.'],['No, averages decide everything.','They can hide disparity.'],['Only after public complaints.','Earlier evidence should be acted on.']]},
{concept:'Mock pacing',prompt:'The simulation has 140 questions and 210 minutes. Average time per question is?',answer:2,options:[['30 seconds.','That uses only 70 minutes.'],['60 seconds.','That uses 140 minutes.'],['90 seconds.','210/140 = 1.5 minutes.'],['210 seconds.','That exceeds the total.']]},
{concept:'Mock interpretation',prompt:'A high practice score comes from familiar bank questions. What limitation should be considered?',answer:3,options:[['It guarantees official passing.','No official threshold is supplied.'],['It proves no gaps remain.','Recognition can hide weak reasoning.'],['It is meaningless in every way.','It still provides practice evidence.'],['Familiarity may inflate the estimate; inspect reasoning and fresh scenarios.','Scores need context.']]},
{concept:'Recovery versus remediation',prompt:'A faulty release is rolled back after sending incorrect notices. What remains?',answer:0,options:[['Identify and correct affected notices/users.','Past effects survive rollback.'],['Nothing; routing erases messages.','It does not.'],['Delete all traces.','Evidence is needed.'],['Only a new model name.','Names do not repair effects.']]},
{concept:'Operational ownership',prompt:'A shared vector store fails and every team says another owns it. What should handover have established?',answer:1,options:[['Only its URL.','An address is not responsibility.'],['Dependency owner, SLO, escalation and recovery procedure.','These make response actionable.'],['Only embedding dimension.','That does not assign operations.'],['No shared components.','Shared services can work with clear ownership.']]}
]});
})();
